Scope of the Reference Implementation

This release includes:

• structural system correctness definitions
• correctness visibility rules
• minimal executable reference (multi-domain structural validation)
• outputs and reference traces

STIC defines a deterministic structural model where system correctness emerges from structure:

resolve(structure) →

• CORRECT if structure is complete AND consistent
• ABSTAIN if structure is incomplete
• BLOCKED if structure is inconsistent

What This Demonstrates

• system correctness without cloud dependency
• independence from centralized validation
• independence from continuous connectivity
• independence from infrastructure as a correctness requirement
• structural resolution governing correctness visibility
• safe handling of incomplete and conflicting structure
• deterministic and reproducible system states
• identical structure produces identical correctness and certificate

Conformance

An implementation is considered compatible if it preserves:

• structure-based correctness
• independence from infrastructure as a requirement for correctness
• deterministic resolution
• structure-driven correctness visibility
• reproducibility of correctness and certificate under equivalent inputs
• explicit handling of incomplete and conflicting structure

Conformance is judged solely by observable behavior — not by internal implementation.

S1 = S2 → Outcome1 = Outcome2 → Certificate1 = Certificate2

Conflict Handling

In this reference implementation:

• BLOCKED is explicitly defined and demonstrated
• ABSTAIN corresponds to incomplete structure (no correctness)
• inconsistent conditions do not produce a visible outcome

Conformance requires:

• no forced correctness under incomplete structure
• no arbitrary correctness under conflicting structure

Normalization Requirement

For deterministic certificate identity:

normalized_outcome = normalize(Outcome)
certificate = hash(normalized_outcome)

Normalization ensures:
• independence from infrastructure state
• independence from representation format
• consistent certificate identity across systems and runs

In the reference implementation:
SHA-256 (truncated) is used for demonstration.

Dependency Independence Guarantee

Correctness is invariant under dependency state:

resolve(S, D1) = resolve(S, D2) for all dependency states D1, D2

This ensures:

dependency_failure != correctness_failure

Correctness depends only on structure — not infrastructure state.

Relationship to Shunyaya Framework

STIC is part of the broader Shunyaya framework.

It builds on:

• SLANG → correctness without execution
• ORL → correctness without ordering
• STIME → correctness without time
• STINT → correctness without connectivity
• STILE → correctness without communication
• SVARE → correctness without computation
• STIC → correctness without cloud dependency

Each removes a dependency.
Correctness remains preserved by structure.

